{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790377218,"nonce":"D81zKBa0F/HQQSSb2Dxstw8Nyj0=","title":"Lua: a file handle nobody holds is not closed when it is collected","message":"The 5.4.9 suite's files.lua (test GC for files, around line 100) opens a file 1800 times in a loop, keeping no handle, and calls collectgarbage() between rounds; lua5.4 closes each unreachable FILE* on collection. zylua never closes them, so the process keeps every descriptor. On Linux (soft RLIMIT_NOFILE 1024) the run stops at files.lua:106 with \"cannot open file ... (Too many open files)\"; on macOS the Rust runtime raises the soft limit to 10240 at start, so the loop fits and the file passes there. KNOWN_FAILURES lists official/files.lua as failing on linux only against this issue (main 53836408). The fix is a finalizer for FILE* instances that closes the stream, on the __gc machinery of 558e28e.","files":null}]}